The most efficient and flexible way to do this in Java is by using a **`StringBuilder`** combined with a **`Pattern`** and **`Matcher`**.

Since you are looking for patterns inside curly braces `{key}`, you can use a Regular Expression to find those keys and replace them with values from your map.

Here is the complete implementation:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.regex.*;

public class StringReplacer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String template = "This is a new {object} at {place}";

        // Create the map of replacements
        Map<String, String> values = new HashMap<>();
        values.put("object", "student");
        values.put("place", "point 3, 4");

        String result = replacePlaceholders(template, values);
        System.out.println(result); 
        // Output: This is a new student at point 3, 4
    }

    public static String replacePlaceholders(String template, Map<String, String> values) {
        // Regex explanation: 
        // \{ matches the literal '{'
        // ([^}]+) captures one or more characters that are NOT '}' (the key)
        // \} matches the literal '}'
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("\\{([^}]+)\\}");
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(template);
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

        while (matcher.find()) {
            // matcher.group(1) is the text inside the curly braces
            String key = matcher.group(1);
            String replacement = values.getOrDefault(key, matcher.group(0));

            // quoteReplacement ensures that special characters in the 
            // replacement string (like $ or \) don't crash the matcher
            matcher.appendReplacement(sb, Matcher.quoteReplacement(replacement));
        }
        matcher.appendTail(sb);

        return sb.toString();
    }

