To achieve this in Next.js, you cannot simply "download" a file into a variable because videos are streamed. Instead, you need to trigger a **browser-level prefetch**.

The most effective way to do this is by creating a **hidden `<video>` element** that loads the source with `preload="auto"`. The browser will then fetch the initial chunks of the video and store them in its internal disk/memory cache.

Here is the complete implementation:

### The Implementation

```tsx
"use client";

import { useEffect } from "react";

interface VideoPrefetchProps {
  url: string;
}

export default function VideoPrefetcher({ url }: VideoPrefetchProps) {
  useEffect(() => {
    // 1. Create a hidden video element
    const video = document.createElement("video");

    // 2. Set essential attributes for caching
    video.src = url;
    video.preload = "auto"; // Tells browser to download as much as possible
    video.muted = true;     // Required for many browsers to allow loading

    // 3. Trigger the load
    video.load();

    // 4. Optional: Stop loading after a few seconds to save user bandwidth
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      video.src = ""; // Clears the source and stops the download stream
      video.load();
    }, 3000); // Cache for 3 seconds

    return () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      video.remove();
    };
  }, [url]);

  // This component renders nothing to the UI
  return null;
}
