If you pass an `async` function into `.map()`, it will return an **array of Promises**, not an array of the resolved values.

Here are the three ways to handle this, depending on whether you want your tasks to run **simultaneously** (fast) or **one-by-one** (sequential).

---

### 1. Parallel Execution (Fastest)
Use this when the order of operations doesn't matter and the tasks don't depend on each other. You use `.map()` to create an array of promises, and then `Promise.all()` to wait for all of them to finish.

```javascript
const ids = [1, 2, 3, 4];

async function fetchData() {
  // 1. .map returns an array of Promises: [Promise, Promise, Promise, Promise]
  const promises = ids.map(async (id) => {
    const response = await fetch(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/${id}`);
    return response.json();
  });

  // 2. Promise.all waits for all promises in the array to resolve
  const results = await Promise.all(promises);

  console.log(results); 
}

fetchData();
```

---

### 2. Sequential Execution (One-by-One)
Use this if you have a rate limit (API throttling) or if **Task 2 depends on the result of Task 1**. 

